{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding","id":1068027,"title":"Dragonblooded Inbreeding","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Dragon-Blooded"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-03-25T09:47:25-07:00","id":1068027,"title":"Dragonblooded Inbreeding","content":"I've always been fascinated by the tragic tales of inbred nobility in European history - people born with immense power and privilege but in some cases horrible physically and mentally deformed, to an extent that I'm not comfortable with even discussing in detail here because it is so disturbing and weird! Why were nobles more inbred than your average common person? I suppose some of these issues come from simple practical concerns of succession and trying to keep rival factions from breaking out, but also from superstition regarding 'blue blood' and the purity of noble lineage. Across human cultures and throughout history, a surprising amount of incest has accompanied most dynasties.<br /> <br /> And this is, of course, in a world without supernatural elemental powers which are bestowed by elemental lineage! Which, you'd have to imagine, would only amplify these superstitious and self-destructive inclinations! So I have to wonder - how much of a problem is inbreeding amongst the noble houses of the Realm? How common are cousin marriages? Sibling marriages? And how common are the kind of afflictions that followed our own real world nobility? Do some houses hold such practices in high esteem while others revile them? Did the Empress marry siblings or cousins forcefully in her attempts to quell dissent or create new alliances?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T10:08:46-07:00","id":1068032,"title":"","content":"My estimation would be either<br /> A) Consanguinuity is common but the detrimental effects are downplayed in patricians and burnt away by Exaltation for the Dragon-Blooded, or<br /> B) They, or the Solars of the First Age if you must, realized full well that varying bloodlines would be more important than inheritances, and marriages tend to be across families rather than inside, barring rare instances, specifically to 'freshen the blood'.<br /> <br /> Mainly because I don't think there's much to be gained from deciding all dragon-blooded bloodlines are one or two bad marriages from Charles II of Spain.<br /> <br /> Far as an authoritative stance goes, I don't own Exalted: the Dragonblooded or other 1e books about them<span style=\"font-size:8px\">, and I wouldn't trust a 2e book not to claim every DB has severe inbred deformities if they'd had the chance to further bolster the edition's pro-Solar obsession</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","user":"Meianno Yuurei","date":". 03-25-2017, 01:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T10:28:06-07:00","id":1068039,"title":"","content":"I think it's not going to be a huge issue for the Scarlet Dynasty because, well, there's 10000 of them. The Kings of medieval Europe often were choosing between 5 or 6 possible matches; there just weren't that many princesses of marriagable age available. At one point Byzantine princesses suddenly became very popular for western European kings; this was because there just weren't enough Catholic princesses available.<br /> <br /> For the Dragonblood, firstly &quot;marriagable age&quot; is referring to people between the ages of about 20 and 80 (after 80, they're nearly all married... though you might well get widows/widowers), and secondly, there's hundreds of eligible bachelors/brides-to-be.<br /> <br /> So it's not difficult for DBs to marry someone who is not their first cousin, and once you get beyond that, inbreeding's not generally a huge problem. And if you do get an inherited condition (like, say, anaemia), then being a Dragonblood may well make you immune to the effects. It's not going to solve the Habsburg chin, but even that was rare amongst medieval monarchs.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T10:50:25-07:00","id":1068043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding#post1068027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always been fascinated by the tragic tales of inbred nobility in European history - people born with immense power and privilege but in some cases horrible physically and mentally deformed, to an extent that I'm not comfortable with even discussing in detail here because it is so disturbing and weird!</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bAre they historical tales?<br /> <br /> \u200bFor the Realm, intermarriage between Exalted and mortals should be kept in mind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T10:58:47-07:00","id":1068046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068039#post1068039\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the Habsburg chin, but even that was rare amongst medieval monarchs.</div> </div> </div> </div>Early Modern Monarchs. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T12:49:30-07:00","id":1068072,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068039#post1068039\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's not going to be a huge issue for the Scarlet Dynasty because, well, there's 10000 of them. The Kings of medieval Europe often were choosing between 5 or 6 possible matches; there just weren't that many princesses of marriagable age available. At one point Byzantine princesses suddenly became very popular for western European kings; this was because there just weren't enough Catholic princesses available.<br /> <br /> For the Dragonblood, firstly &quot;marriagable age&quot; is referring to people between the ages of about 20 and 80 (after 80, they're nearly all married... though you might well get widows/widowers), and secondly, there's hundreds of eligible bachelors/brides-to-be.<br /> <br /> So it's not difficult for DBs to marry someone who is not their first cousin, and once you get beyond that, inbreeding's not generally a huge problem. And if you do get an inherited condition (like, say, anaemia), then being a Dragonblood may well make you immune to the effects. It's not going to solve the Habsburg chin, but even that was rare amongst medieval monarchs.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, keep in mind the Lost Eggs. Yeah, their Breeding isn't high, but it's high enough to sprout an Exaltation; one who chooses to be a soldier instead of a monk can and probably does marry into the Dynasty if she proves herself. Since they're foriegners by definition, that's an infusion of new genes right there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T13:07:07-07:00","id":1068075,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068046#post1068046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Early Modern Monarchs. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>True. In fact inbreeding was a much bigger issue amongst early modern and industrial-era monarchs than amongst medieval monarchs, because of a)the falling number of royal families, and b)Catholic consanginuity laws being ignored by Protestant countries.<br /> <br /> Which again is the point that the number of Dragonblood is probably big enough that I don't think inbreeding's going to be a big problem. It might be an issue if you have several generations only marrying within one of the Houses, but inter-House marriages are very common (and even if they weren't, some of the houses have hundreds of Dragonblood, let alone non-exalted dynasts).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T13:19:11-07:00","id":1068077,"title":"","content":"Keep in mind that the vagaries of genetics, just like the laws of subatomic physics, may not actually map directly to the metaphysical framework of Creation.<br /> <br /> Which is <i>not </i>to say that the Dynasty is just one big incest party. The typical responses from the devs have seemed to suggest that recognizable Earth-norms frequently (though not always) have analogues in Creation, but that the reasons for those norms might be different, non-sciency ones. The underlying forces that discourage inbreeding might not be about &quot;genes&quot; and might instead be something like a religious dictate from the Immaculate Order to maintain the &quot;primal vitality&quot; of bloodlines rather than obsessing over their purity.<br /> <br /> It's also worth mentioning that historically, human marriage between different genetic populations was often (in many places, <i>chiefly</i>) driven not by an awareness that doing so was biologically preferable, but for the sake of establishing social, political, or economic alliances. This was true even outside the elite of a culture - marrying your daughter to a family one village over created a tie between those families, establishing a basis for mutual aid and support later on and strengthening the larger community. Making babies was a socioeconomic imperative as much as it was a biological one. Formal proscription against pairing up with someone inside your own familial sphere is something that we tend to think is squicky these days for all kinds of reasons, only some of which have anything to do with an awareness of things like physical deformities. Really though, if you're a member of a powerful Dynastic family why would you look at a marriagable scion of your House and think &quot;He'd make my cousin happy&quot; instead of &quot;Now what alliances could I cement with his marriage?&quot;<br /> <br /> Since I'm not aware of any canon statements on the issue, there's plenty of room to imagine alternate (or additional) explanations for why inbreeding in the Dynasty is not the norm. But you don't really have to. There are already lots of reasons why doing so is just not as advantageous as the alternative.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Share your wonders in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop#post823884\" target=\"_blank\">The Artifact and Evocation Workshop</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","name":"Ferryman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"960"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T22:20:26-07:00","id":1068183,"title":"","content":"I could see some potential for inbreeding to become a problem if a Civil War does happen. Then, instead of being able to choose from all the houses, you'll be limited to the ones you're currently not fighting AND there are going to be lots of dead DBs everywhere. Granted, I'm sure there will be orders of magnitude more dead soldiers than DBs, but if the war happens, the Houses are going to decimate each other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-25T23:43:11-07:00","id":1068201,"title":"","content":"In my <a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/7628017\" target=\"_blank\">headcanon</a>, 'inbreeding' is a thing for Dragon-Blooded, but only between Dragon-Blooded and mortals (since this story sets the mortals as their mothers) and the deformity is not Exalting.<br /> <br /> Being so close to Gaia, I tell my players that a child born of at least 1 Dragon-Blooded parent, no matter how closely related the parents are, will be born healthy and whole. One of their Exalted powers is breeding, they should be very good at it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wrFXvyXnNjaFJ0XrHdIwce6rnG__0k9/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Sidereal Jumpstart</a> (3e fanwork Charmset)<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DQXwMXm8G1IEYLl-SmuFgKGugeZxQL63/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Cascades</a> (the above in flowchart form)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1103-tikor","name":"Tikor","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"826"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T00:18:47-07:00","id":1068208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068201#post1068201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of their Exalted powers is breeding, they should be very good at it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tell that to the Earth Aspect who got castrated by his sister. <br /> <br /> Personally, I wouldn't describe the ability to pass down my heroic potential through breeding as a &quot;power.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T00:30:00-07:00","id":1068209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding#post1068027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always been fascinated by the tragic tales of inbred nobility in European history - people born with immense power and privilege but in some cases horrible physically and mentally deformed, to an extent that I'm not comfortable with even discussing in detail here because it is so disturbing and weird! Why were nobles more inbred than your average common person? I suppose some of these issues come from simple practical concerns of succession and trying to keep rival factions from breaking out, but also from superstition regarding 'blue blood' and the purity of noble lineage. Across human cultures and throughout history, a surprising amount of incest has accompanied most dynasties.<br /> <br /> And this is, of course, in a world without supernatural elemental powers which are bestowed by elemental lineage! Which, you'd have to imagine, would only amplify these superstitious and self-destructive inclinations! So I have to wonder - how much of a problem is inbreeding amongst the noble houses of the Realm? How common are cousin marriages? Sibling marriages? And how common are the kind of afflictions that followed our own real world nobility? Do some houses hold such practices in high esteem while others revile them? Did the Empress marry siblings or cousins forcefully in her attempts to quell dissent or create new alliances?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Play Crusader Kings and you will realize why Nobles were inbred. They even have an Exalted Dragonblooded mod! :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T03:30:24-07:00","id":1068228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068201#post1068201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my <a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/7628017\" target=\"_blank\">headcanon</a>, 'inbreeding' is a thing for Dragon-Blooded, but only between Dragon-Blooded and mortals</div> </div> </div> </div>Are the quotes a reference to the fact that this isn't inbreeding at all or what is going on?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">(since this story sets the mortals as their mothers) and the deformity is not Exalting.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an unfortunate turn of phrase.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being so close to Gaia, I tell my players that a child born of at least 1 Dragon-Blooded parent, no matter how closely related the parents are, will be born healthy and whole.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, this actually brings me to a certain line of thought concerning genetics and magic.<br /> <br /> \u200bAfter all, genetics is about more than just heredity, it's the core premise of how actual biology functions; at the core of every cell there's a set of key molecules that interact with other molecules in a manner that gives instructions to the cell, particularly in serving as a template for the formation of proteins. Genetic defects tend to take the form of an aberrant sequence that either fails to create key proteins or creates them incorrectly in a manner that tends to have cascade failures throughout the system.<br /> <br /> \u200bSo theoretically, one could have a being where the shortcomings produced by genetic defects are substituted for by magic; for example, somebody with cystic fibrosis still has a defect that leads to not creating a certain protein that would cause the incorrect formation of cell membranes in their throat that leads to accumulations of mucus and other symptoms, just... the magic provides the protein instead.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of their Exalted powers is breeding</div> </div> </div> </div>Nah, I'm pretty sure that every human, barring something unfortunate, can do that.<br /> <br /> \u200bMore seriously, I take the stance that since reproduction is something that is more significant to them, there should be more than a few areas in which they need to treat it carefully to ensure that nothing goes wrong with it. That's another part of why I like the idea that it's unhealthy for the Exalted to have children more frequently than once every five years, because it adds to that perspective of it as something that needs to be understood and handled with care for the health benefits of both baby and mother.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T04:24:42-07:00","id":1068238,"title":"","content":"Notwithstanding what would be best for the imagery The Realm is trying to evoke (e.g. Elric with his frail constitution, and his cousins Cymoril and Yrkoon, etc.), I think Wizard said it first and best here; the size of Dragonblooded population in the Realm, compared to really restricted marriage choices in European royals, would tend shift away from sustained inbreeding.<br /> <br /> (Oddly in a way, the most inbred Dragonblooded might be some threshold petit-dynasties rather than the decadent Realm).<br /> <br /> Even when it comes to cousin marriage, risks do tend to break down after about second cousins, so it's mainly first cousin marriage that is genetically the major issue in a normal population. For the Early Modern European royalty, the issues also were exacerbated by having a buildup of being related in lots of complicated ways (that weren't even always *just* sustained first cousin marriages).<br /> <br /> Tangentially, there are some findings as well recently (with increased ability to measure genetic relatedness well in human population) that friendships tend to form between people who on average about as related as fourth cousins (which is more related than chance) and where reproductive success between partners also seems to peak at third-forth cousin level. (Study populations there are Iceland / United States I believe). This is all very interesting for questions of how genetic kin selection theory has any applicability (if so) to how human society forms and has worked.<br /> <br /> (Of course, however much genetic theory holds in the world of Exalted, and how much it in theory *must* hold for the humanity and life to be as similar to our world as it is, is another potentially very dense question).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T07:00:25-07:00","id":1068280,"title":"","content":"Also, remember that even though it may be OBVIOUS inbreeding is really bad, they may do it purely for religious or spiritual reasons. See the Egyptians here - first sibling marriage, with horrible birth defects, which persisted for centuries to keep the bloodline 'pure'. Sometimes people are just stupid!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T07:58:29-07:00","id":1068293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068280#post1068280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">first sibling</div> </div> </div> </div>As opposed to second sibling or first sibling twice removed? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">with horrible birth defects</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bHmm, I'm reading that some examiners speculate that Tutankhamun's ailments may have been a result of inbreeding, and that in other cases there is limited evidence of defects. Apparently the worst thing that happened for a lot of pharaohs was them being about the same height.<br /> <br /> \u200bI've also read that the genetic analysis of Habsburgs suggest that the infamous chin was not as much of a result of inbreeding as popularly believed.<br /> <br /> \u200bI think with the subject of dynastic inbreeding, it can be difficult to distinguish facts from popular narratives that are likely to have emerged from more recent anti-monarchist sentiments. I'm not saying it's entirely wrong, just something that I try to be a bit sceptical of.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T08:22:04-07:00","id":1068299,"title":"","content":"While the specific nature of Tut's handicaps is unknown to us, it is proven as of a few years ago that his parents were brother and sister, IIRC. The prevalence of incest in Egyptian dynasties is pretty well established as not just being a popular narrative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T08:24:22-07:00","id":1068301,"title":"","content":"And also, remember his dad is thought by many to have been pretty hideously deformed! We're not quite sure whether the depictions of him take artistic license or attempt to convey certain sexual features for political reasons. Anyway, odds are very very high Tut had a few difficulties that may not be immediately obvious from his mummy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T08:36:42-07:00","id":1068306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068299#post1068299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the specific nature of Tut's handicaps is unknown to us, it is proven as of a few years ago that his parents were brother and sister, IIRC. The prevalence of incest in Egyptian dynasties is pretty well established as not just being a popular narrative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Incorrect. <br /> <br /> There is no conclusive evidence who Tutankhamen's parents <i>were</i> -- aside from a talatat block from Amarna naming &quot;King's Son Tutankhuaten&quot;, we have nothing; presumably this refers to Akhenaten, but there is no smoking gun, and we have no connection to any of the possible mother figures confirmed. At all. And the supposed identifications via the DNA analysis rests on accepting the identification of many unlabeled, unnamed mummies to begin with.<br /> <br /> The incest narrative is in fact not &quot;pretty well established&quot; until you reach the Ptolemies. Pops up here and there, but not carrying on for centuries -- Dynasty 18 is certainly no continuous example of it despite your statements here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:courier\">REALITY.SYS corrupt. Reboot Universe? Y/N/Q</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/230-taichara","name":"taichara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar230_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-26T09:16:35-07:00","id":1068322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068299#post1068299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the specific nature of Tut's handicaps is unknown to us, it is proven as of a few years ago that his parents were brother and sister, IIRC.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bBut that doesn't conclusively answer the question of whether or not any ailments he was afflicted with were a consequence of inbreeding.<br /> <br /> \u200bPart of an issue with post hoc assessments of these kinds of monarchs is that they're a small sample size of very prominent figures, and I assert that there can be a tendency towards confirmation bias with them. We look at the examples of European monarchs in the modern era with mental illnesses or severe congenital disorders and scoff at it as the result of inbreeding, with minimal attempt to distinguish it from any other cause or source.<br /> <br /> \u200bThat's what I meant by popular narratives; not the case for marriage between close relatives in cases where it occurred, but the expectation that this led to severely disordered individuals.<br /> <br /> \u200bThat and what I perceive as the subject of inbreeding to start off from a position that associates it with subjects of derision, often involving monarchies or rural people in extreme poverty, the latter particularly often conjuring the image of horrifying deformities. That is another matter I am thinking of when I talk about narratives that obscure reality, even in cases where reality might be similar.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T02:17:34-07:00","id":1068587,"title":"","content":"I remember somewhere in 1st ed is was said that the DB are protected from the inbreeding in some way by the exaltation to an extent.<br /> <br /> I think the point was that if you want to run the inbreeding DB plot then you can, but that its going to be the rarer option because they are exalts and in most cases there are enough of them not to worry about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2325-builder","name":"Builder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T06:18:55-07:00","id":1068635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Builder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068587#post1068587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remember somewhere in 1st ed is was said that the DB are protected from the inbreeding in some way by the exaltation to an extent.</div> </div> </div> </div>Might have to cite that one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T07:09:07-07:00","id":1068651,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3386_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3386\" data-vbnamecard=\"3386\">Piff</a> : is this just a thought exercise, or do you actually want to have physically/mentally deformed Dragonblooded appear in your game as a result of inbreeding?<br /> <br /> Personally, my thinking is that dragonblooded houses are so huge that inbreeding should very rarely be a problem. Even with concerns about inheritance taken into account, the available pool of people should usually be quite large. Genealogy charts might get very complicated due to age differences (e.g. the same set of parents having kids hundreds of years apart, each of whom have comparably sized branch families), but real world bureaucracies have handled worse issues. That, and I don't really want to address themes of incest or birth defects in my game. <br /> <br /> But hey, if you really want to play a Habsburg or Cersei Targaryen, go nuts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-27T07:27:54-07:00","id":1068657,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/dragon-blooded/1068027-dragonblooded-inbreeding?p=1068651#post1068651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3386_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3386\" data-vbnamecard=\"3386\">Piff</a> : is this just a thought exercise, or do you actually want to have physically/mentally deformed Dragonblooded appear in your game as a result of inbreeding?<br /> <br /> Personally, my thinking is that dragonblooded houses are so huge that inbreeding should very rarely be a problem. Even with concerns about inheritance taken into account, the available pool of people should usually be quite large. Genealogy charts might get very complicated due to age differences (e.g. the same set of parents having kids hundreds of years apart, each of whom have comparably sized branch families), but real world bureaucracies have handled worse issues. That, and I don't really want to address themes of incest or birth defects in my game.<br /> <br /> But hey, if you really want to play a Habsburg or Cersei Targaryen, go nuts.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quite possibly - given that the game I'm doing at the moment is Solar focused, the Dragonblooded are a mainly antagonistic force, and they've come to structurally represent archetypes of decadent aristocracy and manifest destiny.<br /> <br /> Thanks once again to everybody in the thread for taking my comprehension of this topic beyond Facebook posts, reruns of Unsolved Mysteries, and Wikipedia. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}